1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:03,369
What's really nice about this desktop app is

2
00:00:03,481 --> 00:00:05,214
that you can of course quickly see which

3
00:00:05,326 --> 00:00:07,716
file was changed and how many lines of code

4
00:00:07,716 --> 00:00:09,754
were added and removed. And you can also

5
00:00:09,802 --> 00:00:11,214
expand this here to

6
00:00:11,824 --> 00:00:14,824
get a detailed view of what was deleted in

7
00:00:14,936 --> 00:00:17,695
red and what was added in green, which is of

8
00:00:17,744 --> 00:00:18,819
course very convenient.

9
00:00:19,749 --> 00:00:22,733
And in addition, in this desktop app here in

10
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:23,583
the top right,

11
00:00:24,161 --> 00:00:25,493
you'll also have this

12
00:00:26,086 --> 00:00:29,135
Div Panel Toggle, and if you click that, you

13
00:00:29,135 --> 00:00:31,333
will get a more detailed view of what

14
00:00:31,382 --> 00:00:33,853
changed here on the right. So I'll quickly

15
00:00:33,885 --> 00:00:36,051
hide my sidebar here on the left to have a

16
00:00:36,051 --> 00:00:36,789
bit more room.

17
00:00:37,415 --> 00:00:39,838
And here you see, you get a list of all the

18
00:00:39,838 --> 00:00:41,940
files that were changed, and of course right

19
00:00:41,972 --> 00:00:42,759
now it's only one.

20
00:00:43,497 --> 00:00:45,326
You can take a look at it and see what was

21
00:00:45,342 --> 00:00:47,653
changed. Now important, this is not what was

22
00:00:47,749 --> 00:00:49,980
changed by the model necessarily. This is

23
00:00:50,076 --> 00:00:52,419
what changed since the last Git commit, and

24
00:00:52,419 --> 00:00:54,505
since I only had an initial commit, the

25
00:00:54,505 --> 00:00:57,697
entire spec.md file is new. So that's the

26
00:00:57,777 --> 00:01:00,581
difference. Here, if I expand this, I see

27
00:01:00,581 --> 00:01:03,641
what the model changed compared to how the

28
00:01:03,641 --> 00:01:05,884
file looked like before the model started.

29
00:01:06,429 --> 00:01:07,983
Here in this preview,

30
00:01:08,512 --> 00:01:10,835
I see what changed compared to the last

31
00:01:10,835 --> 00:01:13,286
commit. Both can of course be useful,

32
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:15,385
especially if you're creating a new commit

33
00:01:15,641 --> 00:01:18,445
after every task finished by a model, which

34
00:01:18,525 --> 00:01:20,576
is generally a good habit to get into.

35
00:01:21,986 --> 00:01:24,325
You can also hide this file tree if you want

36
00:01:24,325 --> 00:01:25,303
a bit more space here.

37
00:01:26,056 --> 00:01:27,821
And you're also able to stage and commit

38
00:01:27,821 --> 00:01:29,601
files from here if you want to. I'll get

39
00:01:29,601 --> 00:01:32,072
back to what you can do with this div view

40
00:01:32,088 --> 00:01:35,264
here. Now, if you're using the command line,

41
00:01:35,697 --> 00:01:38,392
you can open this project in any regular

42
00:01:38,569 --> 00:01:41,296
editor or IDE like Visual Studio Code, and

43
00:01:41,312 --> 00:01:43,509
you could use the built-in source control

44
00:01:43,942 --> 00:01:46,429
panel to see what changed compared to the

45
00:01:46,429 --> 00:01:48,851
last commit. In this case, spec.md is brand

46
00:01:48,851 --> 00:01:49,348
new, so

47
00:01:49,894 --> 00:01:51,787
we don't have any green or red here because

48
00:01:51,819 --> 00:01:52,749
the entire file is new.

49
00:01:53,487 --> 00:01:55,780
And of course, if you would've sent this

50
00:01:55,828 --> 00:01:58,635
prompt through the Codex CLI, it would've

51
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:00,928
also shown you what had changed here in the

52
00:02:01,024 --> 00:02:03,846
output, just as you saw it in that first

53
00:02:03,846 --> 00:02:05,514
example earlier in the course.

54
00:02:06,252 --> 00:02:08,561
Now, here in this native app, you also have

55
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:10,132
3 dots up here,

56
00:02:10,742 --> 00:02:12,955
and you can use that to enable a rich

57
00:02:13,067 --> 00:02:15,088
preview, which shows the rendered markdown

58
00:02:15,152 --> 00:02:17,717
here, for example, which may be a bit easier

59
00:02:17,717 --> 00:02:19,209
to digest and understand.

60
00:02:19,786 --> 00:02:22,771
And here you could also revert this file if

61
00:02:22,771 --> 00:02:24,681
you wanted to and go back to the last

62
00:02:24,681 --> 00:02:27,538
commit. Important, not to the state before

63
00:02:27,586 --> 00:02:29,720
the model started. That could be done

64
00:02:29,736 --> 00:02:30,651
through undo.

65
00:02:31,197 --> 00:02:33,187
But instead here with revert, you would go

66
00:02:33,203 --> 00:02:35,626
back to the last commit for this file.

67
00:02:36,894 --> 00:02:39,221
You can also stage that file to prepare it

68
00:02:39,221 --> 00:02:41,051
to be included in the next commit.

69
00:02:41,612 --> 00:02:43,490
Here at the bottom, you could also revert

70
00:02:43,618 --> 00:02:45,737
all files if multiple files were changed.

71
00:02:45,962 --> 00:02:48,162
Again, going back to the last commit or

72
00:02:48,371 --> 00:02:49,639
stage them all, of course.

73
00:02:50,282 --> 00:02:51,968
And you also have this commit button up

74
00:02:51,968 --> 00:02:55,067
here, which will commit all staged files or

75
00:02:55,356 --> 00:02:57,829
commit and push if you have a remote repo

76
00:02:57,845 --> 00:02:59,853
attached, which I haven't. In that case, you

77
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:01,780
could also create a pull request right away.

78
00:03:03,081 --> 00:03:05,088
So here I'll choose commit because that also

79
00:03:05,152 --> 00:03:07,096
works if you haven't staged files yet, it

80
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:08,702
will then just stage them for you

81
00:03:09,232 --> 00:03:10,870
here with include unstaged.

82
00:03:11,721 --> 00:03:13,568
And if you leave the commit message blank,

83
00:03:13,664 --> 00:03:15,607
the model will generate one for you.

84
00:03:16,972 --> 00:03:19,670
So since I'm happy with my updated spec.md

85
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:20,199
file here,

86
00:03:20,874 --> 00:03:24,165
I will ask it to create a commit, click

87
00:03:24,246 --> 00:03:24,679
Continue,

88
00:03:26,092 --> 00:03:28,180
and it does that. And of course, you don't

89
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:31,455
need the Codex app for that. You could do

90
00:03:31,471 --> 00:03:33,768
that all with good old git add and git

91
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,674
commit commands or by using the integrated

92
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:37,573
version control

93
00:03:38,151 --> 00:03:39,966
tools your IDE might be offering.

94
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:42,599
But of course, having that integrated into

95
00:03:42,647 --> 00:03:44,783
this native app here is kind of convenient

96
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,128
and can make working with this app really

97
00:03:47,261 --> 00:03:47,763
efficient.
